+// Explosion timing (look "boom" 5, time and lifetimes). Everything here runs in
+// simulation time (the step the world simulates by), never in frames.
+//
+// A cased high explosive (shell, grenade, HEAT round) detonates: a flash of
+// tens of milliseconds whose size and duration grow with the cube root of the
+// charge, little lasting flame, then a dark dust and smoke cloud that rises a
+// few tens of metres, spreads, drifts with the wind and lingers many seconds.
+// The charge is read from the ammo's blast (indirectHit times
+// indirectHitRange; a 120 mm HE shell's 150 x 8 = kExplosionRefCharge is the
+// reference), not from its direct hit (a HEAT round's 700 is its penetration:
+// it was drawn as the biggest burst of all). ExplosionVisualSize is the
+// engine's explosion size (Explosion, the combat light's strength):
+// kExplosionRefSize times the cube root of the charge over the reference,
+// kept in [kExplosionMinSize, kExplosionMaxSize] (the old 0.25 .. 1).
+constexpr float kExplosionRefCharge = 1200.0f;
+constexpr float kExplosionRefSize = 0.6f;
+constexpr float kExplosionMinSize = 0.2f;
+constexpr float kExplosionMaxSize = 1.0f;
+float ExplosionVisualSize(float indirectHit, float indirectHitRange);
+// The visible flash of a detonation (s): kExplosionFlashPerSize times the size
+// (a 120 mm HE shell 60 ms, a HEAT round's 38 ms, a hand grenade's 28 ms). Its
+// fireball puffs are born at kFireballBirthK and cool past kFireballFlashEndK
+// (where their glow has fallen under a tenth of a 2200 K puff's) by the end of
+// it: FireballCoolingRate is that rate, K/s, negative (the config's deltaT; the
+// old -4000 for every burst kept a heavy shell's puffs glowing 0.2 s).
+constexpr float kExplosionFlashPerSize = 0.1f;
+constexpr float kFireballBirthK = 2500.0f;
+constexpr float kFireballFlashEndK = 1800.0f;
+float ExplosionFlashSeconds(float size);
+float FireballCoolingRate(float flashSeconds);
+// A vehicle's fuel burns as a deflagration, not a detonation: a large, slower,
+// rolling fireball. Its puffs are born cooler (kFuelFireballK) and cool at
+// kFuelCoolRate K/s, so each glows orange for about a second
+// (FuelFireballGlowSeconds: from birth to kFireballFlashEndK).
+constexpr float kFuelFireballK = 2150.0f;
+constexpr float kFuelCoolRate = -420.0f;
+float FuelFireballGlowSeconds();
+// Its size (the engine's fire size, 0.35 .. 1.25) and how long it keeps
+// feeding (s, 0.3 .. 1.25) grow with the cube root of the fuel's hit (the
+// vehicle's explosives: a Jeep's fuel about 5, a fuel truck's hundreds); under
+// kFuelFireballMinHit nothing burns.
+constexpr float kFuelFireballMinHit = 1.0f;
+float FuelFireballSize(float hit);
+float FuelFireballBurnSeconds(float hit);
+// The dust and smoke column of a crater (CfgCloudlets CraterSmoke1..3; the old
+// puffs held 2 to 2.4 s and were gone 4 s after the burst, most of them by
+// 1.5 s): each puff holds kCraterSmokeHold s and fades over kCraterSmokeFade s,
+// both times the crater's size kept to [0.4, 1.2] (a heavy shell's column about
+// 20 s, a grenade's about 8 s), spreading kCraterSmokeSpread of its size per
+// second and drifting with the wind (kSmokeWindTau). Its rise slows by
+// kCraterSmokeRiseDecay m/s^2 to a slow kCraterSmokeHangRise, so the column
+// stands 15 to 25 m tall, not 60.
+constexpr float kCraterSmokeHold = 9.0f;
+constexpr float kCraterSmokeFade = 9.0f;
+constexpr float kCraterSmokeSpread = 0.08f;
+constexpr float kSmokeWindTau = 3.0f;
+constexpr float kCraterSmokeRiseDecay = -1.1f;
+constexpr float kCraterSmokeHangRise = 0.25f;
+float CraterSmokeLifeScale(float craterSize);
+
 // A fire burns inside the foot of its own smoke, and the light reaching a
 // particle of that smoke has crossed the smoke below it. Soot drinks what it
 // does not scatter: under a burning wreck's black column only the base glows
